NOT a backport — this fix does not exist upstream yet. It is the second shape of
permaweb/HyperBEAM#1062, which opened as GC-before-snapshot; this patch supersedes
`0002-luerl-gc-before-snapshot.patch` and collecting here makes that one redundant,
because the state is already collected by the time anything serializes it. Drop this
patch once the PR lands.

Luerl frees table-store slots only inside `luerl_heap:gc/1` and never collects on its
own, and nothing in HyperBEAM calls it. So the VM state carried between slots in `priv`
retains every table the process has ever allocated: it grows without bound even when
the Lua-visible state is a fixed size, and `dev_lua:snapshot/3` — which serializes that
state twice per slot — grows with it. Measured at v0.9-FINAL on a token contract with a
fixed-size balance map, the snapshot blob went 400,273 B at slot 2 to 20,600,117 B at
slot 150, taking per-message cost with it (537 -> 905 ms).

WHY AFTER COMPUTE RATHER THAN BEFORE SNAPSHOT. Snapshotting collected a COPY, so the
running VM stayed uncollected and only the serialized artifact was bounded. Collecting
in `process_response/3` bounds the live state itself, which is what actually leaks. It
is also deterministic: every node replaying the same messages collects at exactly the
same points, so content-addressed state stays comparable. A collected VM is semantically
identical to an uncollected one but NOT byte-identical, which is why the collect point
has to be a function of the message sequence and nothing else.

WHY UNCONDITIONALLY, NOT ON A COUNTER. Upstream floated rate-limiting the collect
(#1062, options 3-5). Measured on stock `edge` @ 7135fdba with the counter implemented,
across 200 slots of a real contract: GC costs 4.5 ms against ~147 s of compute (0.003%)
and buys a snapshot 20,394x smaller — 16,882 B versus 344,293,708 B — that is also
INVARIANT, since the collect happens inside the compute that made the garbage, so no
checkpoint timing can catch a larger one. Deferring to N=25 admits up to 41.6 MB on 24
slots out of every 25 and saves 3.5 ms of GC per 200 slots. Worst-case pause is 12x
lower collecting every time (60 us versus 750 us). If upstream lands the counter, N=1
reproduces this exactly.

Placement is load-bearing: this is the only quiescent point. `luerl_heap:gc/1` derives
its root set from the state handed to it, so it is sound only where no Lua frame is
live. Here the call has fully returned. DO NOT move it inside the VM or call
`collectgarbage()` from Lua instead — from a `pcall` on the dynamic stack, or during an
in-flight pairs/ipairs over a temporary, live objects are unreachable from the root set
and get freed, surfacing as a raw Erlang {badkey,N} from `luerl_heap:get_env_var/3` or
`luerl_lib_basic:ipairs_next/3`, outside Lua where no pcall can catch it. Measured on
luerl 1.3.0, which edge still vendors (unchanged from v0.9-FINAL).

`MsgResult` is safe to decode before the collect: `luerl:call_function_dec/3` already
decoded it and `decode/2` runs in the `case` head above, so nothing downstream holds a
reference into the collected table store. The error clauses are untouched — a failed
compute discards its state rather than storing it.

Validated for THIS shape (2026-08-09) on an image built from the released patched
image with src/dev_lua.erl reset to stock first, so the collect is the only change:

  D10 E2E                       21/21, and 23/23 with the sustained verticals included
  sustained relay, 10 rounds    39 passed / 0 failed; 2,636 -> 2,779 ms per round (1.05x)
  sustained staking, 10 rounds  40 passed / 0 failed; 3,829 -> 4,206 ms per round (1.10x)
  restart mid-life              full dump identical across a node restart, cold read 169 ms
  store growth                  73 MiB / 50 msgs (relay), 29 MiB / 30 msgs (staking)

Flat per-round latency is the whole point: uncollected, the same workload grows 2.88x
across ten rounds because every slot re-serialises a VM that never stops accumulating.

The blob figures above (400,273 B -> 20,600,117 B) were measured on stock versus the
earlier snapshot-side collect. They are the problem statement, not a measurement of
this patch.

REBASED onto edge @ 14e9f68a (2026-08-26). Upstream reorganised the source tree, so the
file moved `src/dev_lua.erl` -> `src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl`; the `process_response/3`
clause this touches is otherwise BYTE-IDENTICAL to the one at v0.9-FINAL, so the change
itself is unaltered. Still not upstream: `luerl:gc` appears 0 times in edge's dev_lua.erl.

Re-measured on the vanilla edge image (sha256:ed606532) with scripts/qualify-node.ts:
per-message cost growth over 50 accumulated slots was 4.16x, against 1.02x on the
patched v0.9-FINAL image production runs, with the last-10 message average at 2,146 ms
versus 268 ms. Everything else on edge passed unmodified: Tier-2 265/0, all contract
verticals, the staking golden, and restore byte-identical across a restart.

diff --git a/src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl b/src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl
index ca8baf2..bb61c09 100644
--- a/src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl
+++ b/src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl
@@ -349,9 +349,22 @@ process_response({ok, [Status, MsgResult], NewState}, Priv, Opts) ->
     case decode(MsgResult, Opts) of
         Msg when is_map(Msg) ->
             ?event(lua, {response, {status, Status}, {msg, Msg}}),
+            % Garbage-collect the VM before storing it back into `priv'. Luerl
+            % frees table-store slots only inside `luerl_heap:gc/1' and never
+            % collects on its own, so without this the state carried between
+            % slots retains every table the process has ever allocated: it grows
+            % without bound even when the Lua-visible state is a fixed size, and
+            % everything downstream that serializes it (`snapshot/3', which
+            % writes twice per slot) grows with it.
+            %
+            % Here is the right place because it is the only quiescent point:
+            % the Lua call has fully returned, so the root set handed to
+            % `luerl_heap:gc/1' is complete. `MsgResult' was already decoded by
+            % `call_function_dec/3' and again by `decode/2' above, so nothing
+            % downstream holds a reference into the collected store.
             {hb_util:atom(Status), Msg#{
                 <<"priv">> => Priv#{
-                    <<"state">> => NewState
+                    <<"state">> => luerl:gc(NewState)
                 }
             }};
         NonMsgRes -> {hb_util:atom(Status), NonMsgRes}
